1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,738
This tutorial takes cheques from the
office drawer to the

2
00:00:03,738 --> 00:00:07,103
bank, and shows the one journal that
banking writes.

3
00:00:07,103 --> 00:00:10,094
As the Accountant, open Cheque and Cash
Collection.

4
00:00:10,094 --> 00:00:13,832
The Cheque register holds every cheque
from every posted contract.

5
00:00:13,832 --> 00:00:15,701
Filter the status to Registered.

6
00:00:15,701 --> 00:00:19,813
Registered means the cheque is recorded
and its post-dated cheque journal

7
00:00:19,813 --> 00:00:23,552
is written, but the paper is still in the
office.

8
00:00:23,552 --> 00:00:25,047
Now open To deposit.

9
00:00:25,047 --> 00:00:28,411
It lists the registered cheques whose date
has arrived.

10
00:00:28,411 --> 00:00:32,524
Mohammed Al Rashid's cheque 880102 for
19,500 matured on 1 September.

11
00:00:32,524 --> 00:00:34,767
Select it and choose Deposit Batch.

12
00:00:34,767 --> 00:00:37,010
Enter the deposit date and confirm.

13
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:38,879
The cheque moves to Deposited.

14
00:00:38,879 --> 00:00:41,870
Banking writes no journal at all, because
nothing

15
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:44,486
has changed about what the tenant owes.

16
00:00:44,486 --> 00:00:48,599
Back in the register, find Hana Yoshida's
cheque 770109 for 5,500.

17
00:00:48,599 --> 00:00:51,215
It is already Deposited, and today the

18
00:00:51,215 --> 00:00:53,832
bank has confirmed it, so choose Clear.

19
00:00:53,832 --> 00:00:56,823
The dialog shows the account it clears
into,

20
00:00:56,823 --> 00:00:59,814
the Emirates Islamic account for Palm
Ridge Residences.

21
00:00:59,814 --> 00:01:02,057
Confirm, and the cheque is Cleared.

22
00:01:02,057 --> 00:01:03,926
Clearing writes the receipt journal.

23
00:01:03,926 --> 00:01:08,412
Open Journal Voucher and filter to CRT:
the newest one debits the

24
00:01:08,412 --> 00:01:12,524
Emirates Islamic account with 5,500, and
credits PDC Receivable with 5,500.

25
00:01:12,524 --> 00:01:15,889
The money becomes the company's when the
bank pays

26
00:01:15,889 --> 00:01:18,879
it, not when the cheque is handed over.

27
00:01:18,879 --> 00:01:21,870
Finally, look at the summary above the
register.

28
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:25,234
The tiles total what is registered,
deposited and cleared

29
00:01:25,234 --> 00:01:28,599
this month, and the aging strip sorts what
is

30
00:01:28,599 --> 00:01:31,590
due by how many days it is overdue.

31
00:01:31,590 --> 00:01:35,328
What is overdue comes from the register,
cheque by cheque.
